chine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/ml-approaches-for-time-series">ML Approaches for Time Series</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/mse-and-bias-variance-decomposition">MSE and Bias-Variance Decomposition</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/relu">Relu</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/simple-exponential-smoothing">Simple Exponential Smoothing</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tf-idf">TF-IDF</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-optimise-machine-learning-model">How to Optimise Machine Learning Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/multiclass-logistic-regression-from-scratch">Multiclass logistic regression from scratch</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/lightbm-multilabel-classification">Lightbm Multilabel Classification</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/monte-carlo-methods">Monte Carlo Methods</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-inverse-reinforcement-learning">What is Inverse Reinforcement learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/content-based-recommender-system">Content-Based Recommender System</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/context-awareness-recommender-system">Context-Awareness Recommender System</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/predicting-flights-using-machine-learning">Predicting Flights Using Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/ntlk-corpus">NTLK Corpus</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/traditional-feature-engineering-models">Traditional Feature Engineering Models</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/concept-drift-and-model-decay-in-machine-learning">Concept Drift and Model Decay in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/hierarchical-reinforcement-learning">Hierarchical Reinforcement Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-feature-scaling-and-why-is-it-important-in-machine-learning">What is Feature Scaling and Why is it Important in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/difference-between-statistical-model-and-machine-learning">Difference between Statistical Model and Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/introduction-to-ranking-algorithms-in-machine-learning">Introduction to Ranking Algorithms in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/multicollinearity-causes-effects-and-detection">Multicollinearity: Causes, Effects and Detection</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bag-of-n-grams-model">Bag of N-Grams Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tf-idf-model">TF-IDF Model</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/5-probabilistic-training-data-sampling-methods-in-machine-learning">5 Probabilistic Training Data Sampling Methods in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/5-websites-to-download-pre-trained-machine-learning-models">5 Websites to Download Pre-trained Machine Learning Models</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/a-comprehensive-comparison-of-ml-experiment-tracking-tools">A Comprehensive Comparison of ML Experiment Tracking Tools</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/adam-optimizer">Adam Optimizer</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/analytical-solution-in-machine-learning">Analytical Solution in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/analytical-solutions-in-machine-learning">Analytical Solutions in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/arithmetic-geometric-and-harmonic-mean">Arithmetic Geometric and Harmonic Mean</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/assumptions-of-logistic-regression">Assumptions of Logistic Regression</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/attention-mechanism-in-machine-learning">Attention Mechanism in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/autoencoder-classification-in-keras">Autoencoder Classification in Keras</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/automating-api-testing-with-postman">Automating API Testing With Postman</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bag-of-words-bow-model-in-machine-learning">Bag of Words (BoW) Model in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/batch-normalization">Batch Normalization</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/best-books-for-natural-language-processing-with-python">Best Books for Natural Language Processing With Python</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/best-computer-vision-books">Best Computer Vision Books</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/bleu-score-in-machine-learning">Bleu Score in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/building-a-machine-learning-regression-model-from-a-multivariate-nonlinear-dataset">Building a Machine Learning Regression Model from a Multivariate Nonlinear Dataset</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/complex-multi-panel-figures">Complex Multi Panel Figures</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conformal-prediction-for-machine-learning-classification">Conformal Prediction for Machine Learning Classification</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/data-types-in-statistics-used-for-machine-learning">Data Types in Statistics Used for Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/demographic-based-recommender-systems">Demographic-Based Recommender Systems</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/differences-between-machine-learning-engineers-and-researchers">Differences Between Machine Learning Engineers And Researchers</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/distance-measures-for-machine-learning">Distance Measures for Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/early-stopping-in-machine-learning">Early Stopping in Machine Learning</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/f2-score">F2 Score</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/gradient-boosting-algorithm">Gradient Boosting Algorithm</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-do-you-increase-statistical-power">How do you increase Statistical power</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-softmax-activation-function-works">How Softmax Activation Function Works</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/how-to-calculate-correlation-between-variables-in-python">How to Calculate Correlation Between Variables in Python</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/introduction-to-causality-in-machine-learning">Introduction to Causality in Machine Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading4"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse4" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse4" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Related Tutorials</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading4" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse4"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tensorflow">Tensorflow Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch">PyTorch Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/data-science">Data Science Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/nlp">NLP Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reinforcement-learning">Reinforcement Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading5"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse5" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse5" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Interview Questions</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading5" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse5"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/machine-learning-interview-questions">Top 50 Machine learning Interview Questions and Answers (2025)</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="data-structure-for-machine-learning">next →</a> <a class="next" href="how-to-save-a-machine-learning-model">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">Human Activity Recognition Using Smartphone</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>20 Jun 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 19 min read</span></div><p>HAR uses the data from phones or wearables to recognize which physical movements someone is doing. The issue is tough because there is a lot of data to process; it is constantly streaming, and the relationship between what the sensors sense and what is happening is often indirect. Traditional approaches make features by hand, using fixed time windows and models like decision tree ensembles, which need domain knowledge to apply.</p><p>Rather, recent techniques such as recurrent neural networks (<a href="recurrent-neural-network-in-tensorflow">RNN</a>s) and one-dimensional convolutional neural networks (1D CNNs) have reached record-breaking results on HAR tasks even without feature engineering, which makes them easier to apply in more situations.</p><p>In this tutorial, we will use the Activity Recognition Using Smartphones (ARUOS) dataset, which is commonly used in time series classification research. You will pick up the ability to:</p><ul class="points"><li>Give the dataset to the program so it can be examined.</li><li>Try looking at the motion data using line plots, histograms and boxplots.</li><li>Outline the issue clearly, arrange the data, use deep learning models and check how well they perform.</li></ul><h2 class="h2">Understanding Human Activity Recognition</h2><p>HAR means using data from sensors to tell what activity a person is performing. Usually, such activities mean doing typical movements like sitting, standing, walking, jumping or going up and down stairs inside. Usually, sensors known as accelerometers placed somewhere on the body—often on a wearable or smartphone—record motion data across all three axes (x, y, z).</p><p>HAR wants to make sure computers can analyze human actions and respond appropriately.</p><p>Because there is no set general rule that relates information from sensors to types of activities, HAR is a complicated process. It also depends on managing a lot of time-series data, and in the past, it was mainly up to people to create features by using what they understood about the industry.</p><p><a href="what-is-data-analysis">Data analysis</a> for HAR using deep learning has succeeded, automatically finding useful patterns and features so that engineers do not need to design complicated inputs for these systems.</p><h2 class="h2">Smartphone Data for Activity Recognition</h2><p>In 2012, Davide Anguita and colleagues from the University of Genova, Italy, created the Activity Recognition Using Smartphones dataset, which has become popular for measuring human activity recognition. You can read a full explanation in their 2013 paper, "A Public Domain Dataset for Human Activity Recognition Using Smartphones," while their 2012 research modelled it as a multiclass, hardware-efficient support vector machine (SVM).</p><p>The “UCI <a href="machine-learning">Machine Learning</a> Repository” provides the dataset for free.</p><h3 class="h3">Data Collection</h3><p>Data was taken from a group of 30 participants who were between 19 and 48 years old.</p><p>While doing six activities, the subjects all wore a Samsung Galaxy S II smartphone around their waist.</p><ul class="points"><li>Walking</li><li>Walking Upstairs</li><li>Walking Downstairs</li><li>Sitting</li><li>Standing</li><li>Laying</li></ul><p>A smartphone with an accelerometer and gyroscope collected data from the sensors every second at a rate of 50 Hz (acceleration data along the three axes and rotational speed data along the x, y and z axes).</p><p>Each task was tested each time with the phone in a different position, always left then right.</p><p>Videos captured all the activities, and afterward, they were double-checked and labeled for accuracy.</p><h3 class="h3">Data Preprocessing</h3><p>The dataset does not have all the raw information captured by the sensors. It does not send raw data; it first processes the data through a series of steps:</p><ul class="points"><li>Added noise filters to the data coming from the accelerometer and gyroscope.</li><li>Divide the data into windows of 2.56 seconds (128 readings per window) and make sure the next window overlaps with the previous one by 50 per cent.</li><li>Parted the accelerometer data into the effects of movement and gravity on the body.</li><li>Five hundred sixty-one features were extracted from each window using the usual time-domain and frequency-domain methods.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Dataset Format</h3><p>Among the dataset, 70% is allocated for training and 30% for testing, and this division is based on the data and IDs of the 20 participants (21 for training, 9 for testing), guaranteeing that each participant appears only once in a single set.</p><p>The purpose of modelling is to guess which activity will occur in the next 2.56 seconds based on the engineered features.</p><h3 class="h3">Initial Results</h3><p>Applying mobile-suitable SVMs (with fixed-point arithmetic), the authors managed to attain an accuracy of 89% on the test set, and their results were similar to those of traditional SVM implementations.</p><p>This data has now become a main guideline for checking how machine learning and <a href="deep-learning">deep learning</a> techniques perform on human activity recognition tasks.</p><h2 class="h2">Download the Dataset</h2><p>People can get the Activity Recognition Using Smartphones dataset by downloading it from the UCI Machine Learning Repository.</p><p>It is provided in the form of a ZIP archive (58 MB): <strong>The UCI HAR Dataset.zip file</strong>.</p><p>When the download is finished, place all the contents into a folder called "HARDataset" within your working directory.</p><h3 class="h3">How are the folders created in the file structure?</h3><p>Once gathering is done, the dataset contains the following:</p><p>A README.txt file that explains in detail what the dataset contains and information about each of the files.</p><p>There is a features.txt file containing descriptions of all 561 engineered features.</p><p>Bin and Networking are the two main folders:</p><ul class="points"><li>Divide the data into 70% for training (training/validation set).</li><li>During testing (with 30% of the data), compare the model’s predictions to the correct answers to find out how well the model handles observations it has never seen before.</li></ul><p>There is a set of documents inside every folder:</p><ul class="points"><li>X_train.txt / X_test.txt: Data that is created from multiple features of each entry.</li><li>y_train.txt / y_test.txt: The labels each activity is given in the dataset (1–6).</li><li>Subject IDs (1–30) are found in these files.</li><li>Inertial Signals/(Raw Energy Data): Just-processed information from the sensors.</li></ul><h3 class="h3">Inertial Signals List</h3><p>Inside the Inertial Signals subdirectory, you'll find nine files per set (train/test):</p><ul class="points"><li>Total acceleration (gravitational + body motion):</li></ul><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">total_acc_x_*.txt, total_acc_y_*.txt, total_acc_z_*.txt    
</textarea></div><ul class="points"><li>Body acceleration (motion only):</li></ul><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">body_acc_x_*.txt, body_acc_y_*.txt, body_acc_z_*.txt    
</textarea></div><ul class="points"><li>Body gyroscope (angular velocity):</li></ul><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">body_gyro_x_*.txt, body_gyro_y_*.txt, body_gyro_z_*.txt    
</textarea></div><p>There are columns in each file, separated with whitespace, and the values are between -1 and 1, as described in README.txt.</p><h3 class="h3">A Particular Point of the Study</h3><p>This tutorial will be about the raw, preprocessed sensor data found in the Inertial Signals folders. These files can directly train machine learning models on the data, which means you don't have to engineer the features yourself.</p><h2 class="h2">Loading Data to the Database</h2><p>We will write code here to put the HAR dataset into memory so we can model it.</p><h3 class="h3">Step 1: Open a Single File</h3><p>We can start by loading a single sensor data file using pandas.read_csv() with header=None and delim_whitespace=True since the files are whitespace-separated and have no headers:</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">from pandas import read_csv

# Load a single file and return as a NumPy array
def load_file(filepath):
    dataframe = read_csv(filepath, header=None, delim_whitespace=True)
    return dataframe.values
</textarea></div><p><strong>Example Usage:</strong></p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">data = load_file('HARDataset/train/Inertial Signals/total_acc_y_train.txt') 
print(data.shape)  
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><div class="codeblock3"><pre> (7352, 128)
