</textarea></div><p>Data points are all 128-time steps long and consist of 9 features (from all sensor axes). Every sample is assigned an activity label from the standard classes.</p><h2 class="h2">HAR Dataset Class Balance Check</h2><p>Before training any model, we also need to make sure that the dataset is balanced (i.e., each activity class is represented with roughly the same number of examples). The balanced dataset is used for some reason as it helps to avoid bias during training or, for example, to give better generalization.</p><p>The 30 subjects should have completed all six activities. Checking this assumption also makes sure that we did not miss anything in the data-loading step.</p><h3 class="h3">Summarize Class Distribution Function</h3><p>We define a helper function class_breakdown() that:</p><ul class="points"><li>Takes a NumPy array as input and converts it into a Pandas DataFrame</li><li>Creates the groups from the data that are distinguished by the class label.</li><li>It prints the count and percentage of each class.</li></ul><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">from pandas import DataFrame

# Summarize the balance of classes in a dataset
def class_breakdown(data):
    df = DataFrame(data)
    class_counts = df.groupby(0).size().values
    for i in range(len(class_counts)):
        percent = class_counts[i] / len(df) * 100
        print(f'Class={i+1}, total={class_counts[i]}, percentage={percent:.3f}')
</textarea></div><h3 class="h3">Load Labels and Check Balance</h3><p>Now, we load the training and test labels, and individually, we summarize the class distributions, and then we check the combined breakdown.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">from numpy import vstack
