# load a list of files, such as x, y, z data for a given variable
def load_group(filenames, prefix=''):
	loaded = list()
	for name in filenames:
		data = load_file(prefix + name)
		loaded.append(data)
	# Stack group so that features are the 3rd dimension
	loaded = dstack(loaded)
	return loaded

# Load a dataset group, such as train or test
def load_dataset(group, prefix=''):
	filepath = prefix + group + '/Inertial Signals/'
	# Load all nine files as a single array
	filenames = list()
	# total acceleration
	filenames += ['total_acc_x_'+group+'.txt', 'total_acc_y_'+group+'.txt', 'total_acc_z_'+group+'.txt']
	# body acceleration
	filenames += ['body_acc_x_'+group+'.txt', 'body_acc_y_'+group+'.txt', 'body_acc_z_'+group+'.txt']
	# body gyroscope
	filenames += ['body_gyro_x_'+group+'.txt', 'body_gyro_y_'+group+'.txt', 'body_gyro_z_'+group+'.txt']
	# Load input data
	X = load_group(filenames, filepath)
	# load class output
	y = load_file(prefix + group + '/y_'+group+'.txt')
	return X, y

# get all data for one subject
def data_for_subject(X, y, sub_map, sub_id):
	# get row indexes for the subject id
	ix = [i for i in range(len(sub_map)) if sub_map[i]==sub_id]
	# return the selected samples
	return X[ix, :, :], y[ix]

# convert a series of windows to a 1D list
def to_series(windows):
	series = list()
	for window in windows:
		# remove the overlap from the window
		half = int(len(window) / 2) - 1
		for value in window[-half:]:
			series.append(value)
	return series

# group data by activity
def data_by_activity(X, y, activities):
	# Group windows by activity
	return {a:X[y[:,0]==a, :, :] for a in activities}

# Plot activity durations by subject
def plot_activity_durations_by_subject(X, y, sub_map):
	# get unique subjects and activities
	subject_ids = unique(sub_map[:,0])
	activity_ids = unique(y[:,0])
	# enumerate subjects
	activity_windows = {a:list() for a in activity_ids}
	for sub_id in subject_ids:
		# get data for one subject
		_, subj_y = data_for_subject(X, y, sub_map, sub_id)
		# count windows by activity
		for a in activity_ids:
			activity_windows[a].append(len(subj_y[subj_y[:,0]==a]))
	# organize durations into a list of lists
	durations = [activity_windows[a] for a in activity_ids]
	pyplot.boxplot(durations, labels=activity_ids)
	pyplot.show()

# load training dataset
X, y = load_dataset('train', 'HARDataset/')
# load mapping of rows to subjects
sub_map = load_file('HARDataset/train/subject_train.txt')
# plot durations
plot_activity_durations_by_subject(X, y, sub_map)
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><img alt="Human Activity Recognition Using Smartphone" src="https://images.tpointtech.com/tutorial/machine-learning/images/human-activity-recognition-using-smartphone4.png"/><h2 class="h2">Modelling Approach</h2><p>The section lays out important strategies for using the activity recognition dataset, grouping them by main components of a project like framing the issue, preparing data, making the model and evaluating it.</p><h3 class="h3">Problem Framing</h3><p>The goal is to find out what a subject is doing based on a movement window after learning from other data sets. This way of testing is like how people really use an application. Alternative approaches for framing could be using only a single moment in time, sequences of windows or noticing activity transitions. Some visualizations may not be quite right, but they give useful hints when looking at the data.</p><h3 class="h3">Data Preparation</h3><p>Even though the data is [-1, 1], there are still useful preprocessing techniques. You need to normalize or standardize your signals (for every subject or globally), choose which features to keep (by axis or sensor type) and eliminate outliers. Changing the number of samples by oversampling or downsampling and balancing the sampling rate is another way to advance learning.</p><h3 class="h3">Predictive Modeling</h3><p>It is a multiclass classification problem dealing with time series data. The original work started by using engineered features together with an SVM for comparison. Other ML methods worth trying are regular ML algorithms, MLPs, 1D CNNs, LSTMs and hybrid styles like CNNs with LSTMs, particularly for using raw data.</p><h3 class="h3">Model Evaluation</h3><p>The dataset was first divided so that 70% of the data was used for training and the rest for testing. One better way to do cross-validation is subject, as it allows analysis of all subjects. Accuracy and a <a href="confusion-matrix-in-machine-learning">confusion matrix</a> should be part of the evaluation, as these show what activities are mistakenly classified as others.</p><h2 class="h2">Conclusion</h2><p>You worked with the Activity Recognition Using Smartphones Dataset to classify time series data and also saw how to prepare the data for modelling. You were able to download and load the motion dataset and used line plots, histograms and boxplots to look at the structure and arrangement of the data. In the end, you gained skills on how to move forward in the modelling process, which means framing the problem, preparing data, selecting the best models and evaluating their results.</p><hr/><div class="nexttopicdiv"><span class="nexttopictext">Next Topic</span><span class="nexttopiclink"><a href="data-structure-for-machine-learning">Data Structure for Machine Learning</a></span></div><br/> <div id="bottomnext"><a class="next" href="how-to-save-a-machine-learning-model" style="float:left">← prev</a> <a class="next" href="data-structure-for-machine-learning" style="float:right">next →</a></div><br/> </td></tr></tbody></table></div></body></html><br/></div><div class="related-posts-container"><h2 class="h3">Related Posts</h2><ul class="related-posts-list"><li><a class="related-post-item" href="c-gan"><h3 class="h3">C Gan</h3><p class="related-post-content">Conditional Generative Adversarial Networks (cGANs) are an extension of regular GANs that incorporate conditional information during the training phase. The generator in a conventional GAN generates data samples with no control over the exact qualities of the produced output. CGANs, on the other hand, enable the...</p><p class="reading-time"><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 10 min read <span class="arrow-position"><svg fill="currentColor" height="1.3rem" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0" viewbox="0 0 512 512" width="1.3rem" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M295.6 163.7c-5.1 5-5.1 13.3-.1 18.4l60.8 60.9H124.9c-7.1 0-12.9 5.8-12.9 13s5.8 13